    {
      "node_id": 1,
      "seq_id": 0,
      "kind": "content",
      "text": " Transformers are slow and memory-hungry on long sequences, since the time and memory complexity of self-attention are quadratic in sequence length. Approximate attention methods have attempted to address this problem by trading off model quality to reduce the compute complexity, but often do not achieve wall-clock speedup. We argue that a missing principle is making attention algorithms ",
      "n_tokens_est": 67,
      "pos_start_est": 5
    },
    {
      "node_id": 1,
      "seq_id": 0,
      "kind": "markup",
      "text": "\\textit",
      "n_tokens_est": 2,
      "pos_start_est": 72
    },
    {
      "node_id": 1,
      "seq_id": 0,
      "kind": "content",
      "text": "IO-aware",
      "n_tokens_est": 3,
      "pos_start_est": 74
    },
    {
      "node_id": 1,
      "seq_id": 0,
      "kind": "markup",
      "text": "---",
      "n_tokens_est": 3,
      "pos_start_est": 77
    },
    {
      "node_id": 1,
      "seq_id": 0,
      "kind": "content",
      "text": "accounting for reads and writes between levels of GPU memory. We propose ",
      "n_tokens_est": 13,
      "pos_start_est": 80
    },
    {
      "node_id": 1,
      "seq_id": 0,
      "kind": "markup",
      "text": "\\sysname",
      "n_tokens_est": 2,
      "pos_start_est": 93
    },
    {
      "node_id": 1,
      "seq_id": 0,
      "kind": "content",
      "text": ", an IO-aware exact attention algorithm that uses tiling to reduce the number of memory reads/writes between GPU high bandwidth memory (HBM) and GPU on-chip SRAM. We analyze the IO complexity of ",
      "n_tokens_est": 41,
      "pos_start_est": 95
    },
    {
      "node_id": 1,
      "seq_id": 0,
      "kind": "markup",
      "text": "\\sysname",
      "n_tokens_est": 2,
      "pos_start_est": 136
    },
    {
      "node_id": 1,
      "seq_id": 0,
      "kind": "content",
      "text": ", showing that it requires fewer HBM accesses than standard attention, and is optimal for a range of SRAM sizes. We also extend ",
      "n_tokens_est": 25,
      "pos_start_est": 138
    },
    {
      "node_id": 1,
      "seq_id": 0,
      "kind": "markup",
      "text": "\\sysname",
      "n_tokens_est": 2,
      "pos_start_est": 163
    },
    {
      "node_id": 1,
      "seq_id": 0,
      "kind": "content",
      "text": "to block-sparse attention, yielding an approximate attention algorithm that is faster than any existing approximate attention method. ",
      "n_tokens_est": 21,
      "pos_start_est": 165
    },
    {
      "node_id": 1,
      "seq_id": 0,
      "kind": "markup",
      "text": "\\sysname",
      "n_tokens_est": 2,
      "pos_start_est": 186
    },
    {
      "node_id": 1,
      "seq_id": 0,
      "kind": "content",
      "text": "trains Transformers faster than existing baselines: 15",
      "n_tokens_est": 8,
      "pos_start_est": 188
    },
    {
      "node_id": 1,
      "seq_id": 0,
      "kind": "markup",
      "text": "\\%",
      "n_tokens_est": 2,
      "pos_start_est": 196
    },
    {
      "node_id": 1,
      "seq_id": 0,
      "kind": "content",
      "text": " end-to-end wall-clock speedup on BERT-large (seq.",
      "n_tokens_est": 16,
      "pos_start_est": 198
    },
    {
      "node_id": 1,
      "seq_id": 0,
      "kind": "markup",
      "text": "\\ ",
      "n_tokens_est": 1,
      "pos_start_est": 214
    },
    {
      "node_id": 1,
      "seq_id": 0,
      "kind": "content",
      "text": "length 512) compared to the MLPerf 1.1 training speed record, 3",
      "n_tokens_est": 15,
      "pos_start_est": 215
    },
    {
      "node_id": 1,
      "seq_id": 0,
      "kind": "content",
      "text": "$\\times$",
      "n_tokens_est": 4,
      "pos_start_est": 230
    },
    {
      "node_id": 1,
      "seq_id": 0,
      "kind": "content",
      "text": " speedup on GPT-2 (seq.",
      "n_tokens_est": 8,
      "pos_start_est": 234
    },
    {
      "node_id": 1,
      "seq_id": 0,
      "kind": "markup",
      "text": "\\ ",
