      "pos_start_est": 776
    },
    {
      "node_id": 2,
      "seq_id": 1,
      "kind": "markup",
      "text": "\\citep",
      "n_tokens_est": 2,
      "pos_start_est": 777
    },
    {
      "node_id": 2,
      "seq_id": 1,
      "kind": "content",
      "text": "williams2009roofline, hennessy2003memory",
      "n_tokens_est": 3,
      "pos_start_est": 779
    },
    {
      "node_id": 2,
      "seq_id": 1,
      "kind": "content",
      "text": ". However, common Python interfaces to deep learning such as PyTorch and Tensorflow do not allow fine-grained control of memory access. We propose ",
      "n_tokens_est": 27,
      "pos_start_est": 782
    },
    {
      "node_id": 2,
      "seq_id": 1,
      "kind": "markup",
      "text": "\\sysname",
      "n_tokens_est": 2,
      "pos_start_est": 809
    },
    {
      "node_id": 2,
      "seq_id": 1,
      "kind": "content",
      "text": ", a new attention algorithm that computes exact attention with far fewer memory accesses. Our main goal is to avoid reading and writing the attention matrix to and from HBM. This requires (i) computing the softmax reduction without access to the whole input (ii) not storing the large intermediate attention matrix for the backward pass. We apply two well-established techniques to address these challenges. (i) We restructure the attention computation to split the input into blocks and make several passes over input blocks, thus incrementally performing the softmax reduction (also known as ",
      "n_tokens_est": 106,
      "pos_start_est": 811
    },
    {
      "node_id": 2,
      "seq_id": 1,
      "kind": "markup",
      "text": "\\textbf",
      "n_tokens_est": 2,
      "pos_start_est": 917
    },
    {
      "node_id": 2,
      "seq_id": 1,
      "kind": "content",
      "text": "tiling",
      "n_tokens_est": 1,
      "pos_start_est": 919
    },
    {
      "node_id": 2,
      "seq_id": 1,
      "kind": "content",
      "text": "). (ii) We store the softmax normalization factor from the forward pass to quickly ",
      "n_tokens_est": 17,
      "pos_start_est": 920
    },
    {
      "node_id": 2,
      "seq_id": 1,
      "kind": "markup",
      "text": "\\textbf",
      "n_tokens_est": 2,
      "pos_start_est": 937
    },
    {
      "node_id": 2,
      "seq_id": 1,
      "kind": "content",
      "text": "recompute",
      "n_tokens_est": 1,
      "pos_start_est": 939
    },
    {
      "node_id": 2,
      "seq_id": 1,
      "kind": "content",
      "text": " attention on-chip in the backward pass, which is faster than the standard approach of reading the intermediate attention matrix from HBM. We implement ",
      "n_tokens_est": 27,
      "pos_start_est": 940
    },
    {
      "node_id": 2,
      "seq_id": 1,
      "kind": "markup",
      "text": "\\sysname",
      "n_tokens_est": 2,
      "pos_start_est": 967
    },
    {
      "node_id": 2,
      "seq_id": 1,
      "kind": "content",
      "text": "in CUDA to achieve fine-grained control over memory access and fuse all the attention operations into one GPU kernel. Even with the increased FLOPs due to recomputation, our algorithm both ",
      "n_tokens_est": 34,
      "pos_start_est": 969
    },
    {
      "node_id": 2,
      "seq_id": 1,
      "kind": "markup",
      "text": "\\textbf",
      "n_tokens_est": 2,
      "pos_start_est": 1003
    },
    {
      "node_id": 2,
      "seq_id": 1,
      "kind": "content",
      "text": "runs faster",
      "n_tokens_est": 2,
      "pos_start_est": 1005
    },
    {
      "node_id": 2,
      "seq_id": 1,
      "kind": "content",
      "text": " (up to 7.6x on GPT-2",
      "n_tokens_est": 10,
      "pos_start_est": 1007
    },
    {
      "node_id": 2,
      "seq_id": 1,
      "kind": "markup",
      "text": "~",
      "n_tokens_est": 1,
      "pos_start_est": 1017
    },
    {
